∑O¶[üŸ∏•∞±§§V)ßÌ∂¶?Í|ﬂWX˚VêWó{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A definition type lets a host or library describe a family of named\nfunctions. The language checks each definition against a shared signature,\nwhile the host can discover and invoke definitions by name. This is the\nmechanism used by integrations such as Pekit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"embedding-definition-types--a-family-of-definitions\">A family of definitions <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#embedding-definition-types--a-family-of-definitions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Mark a public function with <code>#[def_type]</code>. Its first parameter receives the\ndefinition's path as a string; its second receives the definition function.\nFurther parameters become invocation arguments. This example wraps a\npure string-producing definition:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[def_type]\ndef describe(name: String, body: fn(): String): String {\n    \"{name}: {body()}\"\n}\ndef describe dev.peios.gcsl() { \"language tools\" }\nresult = describe dev.peios.gcsl()\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Declaring the definition does not execute its body. Calling its reference\ndoes. Definitions live in the definition type's namespace, separate from\nordinary values of the same path. Their public paths merge through\nworkspace directories and imports like other public declarations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"embedding-definition-types--definitions-made-with-a-variant\">Definitions made with a variant <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#embedding-definition-types--definitions-made-with-a-variant\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A definition type can take a variant of an <a href=\"#language-records-and-types\">enum</a>\nafter the name, and the definition after that. Each definition then\nnames a variant, and each variant has its own definitions. When the\ndefinition type is generic, the variant's payload type fills in its type\nparameter, so each definition's body is checked against its own\nvariant's payload:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Tool extensible&lt;{*: String}&gt;\nenum Tool::C&lt;{cc: String}&gt;\nenum Tool::Rust&lt;{rustc: String}&gt;\n#[def_type]\ndef Tool&lt;T&gt;(name: String, kind: Variant&lt;Tool, T&gt;, body: fn(): T): Tool {\n    kind(body())\n}\ndef Tool::C gcc() { cc = \"gcc\" }\ndef Tool::Rust gcc() { rustc = \"rustc\" }\nc = Tool::C gcc()\nrust = Tool::Rust gcc()\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>Tool::C gcc</code> and <code>Tool::Rust gcc</code> are separate definitions. A reference\nnames the variant in the same way as the definition, and the definition\ntype receives the variant as its <code>kind</code> argument. Hosts don't invoke these\ndefinitions by name: programs reach them through references, for example\nin a list of registered toolchains.</p>\n<h2 id=\"embedding-definition-types--metadata\">Metadata <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#embedding-definition-types--metadata\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Function types can carry metadata with <code>META:</code>, <code>META-&gt;</code>, or <code>META-&gt;&gt;</code>.\nThese distinguish pure reads, impure reads, and memoized reads. A\n<code>defmeta</code> declaration supplies the metadata of a definition in its\ndirectory. Reading a field through the function reference reads that\nmetadata according to its effect.</p>\n<p>Metadata is not a separate package-format contract. The definition type\nchooses its shape, and the host integration owns what those fields mean.\nUse the host's documentation for package or build semantics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"embedding-definition-types--host-invocation\">Host invocation <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#embedding-definition-types--host-invocation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A checked workspace exposes definition types and their definitions. Host\nAPIs can invoke a selected definition with named textual arguments parsed\nagainst its declared parameter types. When a value the host gets back holds\na reference, such as <code>build = build busybox</code>, the host can ask which\ndefinition it names and then invoke it, or read its metadata, by name. The\nhost can also invoke a definition in a <a href=\"#language-context\">context</a> of its\nchoosing, such as the platform a build step runs on, by giving each\ncontext a value of its declared type, for example one it got back from\nan earlier run. Only that invocation sees those values: what each file\ncomputes as it runs uses the declared defaults. The\n<code>gcsl</code> executable itself exposes\n<code>run</code>, <code>check</code>, <code>fmt</code>, and <code>lsp</code>; it does not add a general command for each\ndefinition type.</p>\n<p>Keep protocol or package policy in the integration repository. Keep\nlanguage syntax, type checking, and discovery semantics in GCSL. This\nseparation lets other hosts use the same language without inheriting\nPekit's packaging model.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"embedding-definition-types--a-family-of-definitions","title":"A family of definitions","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"embedding-definition-types--definitions-made-with-a-variant","title":"Definitions made with a variant","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"embedding-definition-types--metadata","title":"Metadata","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"embedding-definition-types--host-invocation","title":"Host invocation","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[["gcsl/context",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/context","Context"]}],["gcsl/records-and-types",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/records-and-types","Records and Types"]}]],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}